rce and it's been  split up into several portions so let's  say a triplicate or a duplicate the  reason why you're doing is is that with  the biological replicates  you are controlling your hypothesis so  your experimental hypothesis  whereas with your technical replicate  you are controlling your process in this  respect let's say your pipetting for  example or your your precision in  general  thank you for that clarification Tim now  how do I know which setting to choose  for my liquid when I am using a robot  um yeah that is also a pretty pretty  good question so it brings me back to  the slide and maybe I can quickly go to  that slide where I was showing a  different pipetting parameter  I think it's that like yes so first of  all yes you can you can go with the  Dupree setting that I think it's the  easiest way at least for the for the  ethanol system so if you are using PCR  premix go for PCR premix if you're using  glycerol go for I thought so but then  you can also change a lot of the  parameters manually so the expiration  speeds the dispensing speed and stuff  like that and I have to tell you and to  my understanding it's not a very exact  science it is a little bit of trial and  error but there are some rules of thumb  so for example if you have something  which is very viscous I would go for a  very slow expiration speed and a very  slow distending speed and I would also  use a blowout so I would prefer a  pipetting mode over a mighty dispense  mode because the blood can kick out this  last little bit of liquid and from your  tip for example if you work with  something which is very volatile um I  would always go for pre vetting because  with this pre vetting you avoid dripping  with your offshore liquid out of your  out of your tips anyhow if you are not  sure what are the best settings for your  liquid you can always come back to us  contact us here at Eppendorf we have a  test lab we can even test that for you  and so we can support you find the  sett